BEGIN;
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit technologist compares ''positive'' yarn feed systems (pre-measuring exact yarn length per course under controlled tension, similar in principle to the earlier weaving positive-feeder discussion) against simple gravity/tension-controlled feed on a circular knitting machine. Why does positive feed become INCREASINGLY important as machine speed and feeder count both increase?', '["Positive feed has no relationship to machine speed or feeder count, making this described increasing importance technically unfounded","At higher speeds and with MORE simultaneous feeders (each drawing yarn independently), maintaining CONSISTENT tension across every single feeder becomes progressively harder with simple gravity/tension-controlled feed, since small package-to-package or feeder-to-feeder tension variations compound into visible loop-length inconsistency (connecting to the earlier loop-length-consistency/GSM and barre-defect discussions) — positive feed''s precise, controlled length-metering becomes proportionally more valuable as the NUMBER of simultaneous tension-sensitive variables (feeders) and the SPEED (less time for tension to self-correct) both increase","Simple gravity/tension-controlled feed always becomes MORE reliable and consistent at higher speeds and feeder counts, the opposite of the actual relationship","Positive feed systems can only be used on machines with a single feeder, making this comparison technically inapplicable to any multi-feeder circular knitting machine"]'::jsonb, 'At higher speeds and with MORE simultaneous feeders (each drawing yarn independently), maintaining CONSISTENT tension across every single feeder becomes progressively harder with simple gravity/tension-controlled feed, since small package-to-package or feeder-to-feeder tension variations compound into visible loop-length inconsistency (connecting to the earlier loop-length-consistency/GSM and barre-defect discussions) — positive feed''s precise, controlled length-metering becomes proportionally more valuable as the NUMBER of simultaneous tension-sensitive variables (feeders) and the SPEED (less time for tension to self-correct) both increase', 'This connects the earlier positive-feed, loop-length-consistency, and barre-defect discussions into a scaling argument — with MORE simultaneous feeders, there are proportionally MORE independent tension variables that could each drift slightly differently, and at HIGHER speed, there''s less time for any self-correcting tension behavior to occur — both factors compound the risk of feeder-to-feeder loop-length/tension inconsistency (directly causing the earlier-discussed barre-type defects and GSM variation) with simple gravity-fed systems, which is precisely why high-speed, high-feeder-count modern circular knitting machines have made positive feed technology essentially a standard requirement rather than an optional refinement.', 3, '{"positive-feed-scaling"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A knit fabric engineer explains that a fabric''s ELASTIC RECOVERY (how completely it returns to original dimension after stretching) tends to DEGRADE gradually with each additional wash cycle, even for spandex-containing fabric. What underlying mechanism most directly explains this progressive recovery degradation over repeated washing?', '["Elastic recovery never degrades with washing under any circumstances, making the premise of this observed gradual degradation technically incorrect","Repeated mechanical stress (stretching/relaxing cycles during wash agitation) combined with sustained exposure to heat, moisture, and detergent chemistry can gradually cause fatigue/degradation in the elastane polymer''s molecular network (the same type of elastomeric cross-linked structure discussed earlier for spandex production) — each wash cycle contributes a small amount of cumulative mechanical and chemical fatigue, progressively reducing the elastane''s ability to fully recover to its original relaxed state, even though this effect isn''t usually visible after just one or a few washes","Elastic recovery degradation is caused entirely by the base (non-elastane) fiber content, with zero contribution from the elastane component itself","Wash-induced elastic recovery degradation only affects garments containing zero elastane, making this scenario''s premise about a spandex-containing fabric technically contradictory"]'::jsonb, 'Repeated mechanical stress (stretching/relaxing cycles during wash agitation) combined with sustained exposure to heat, moisture, and detergent chemistry can gradually cause fatigue/degradation in the elastane polymer''s molecular network (the same type of elastomeric cross-linked structure discussed earlier for spandex production) — each wash cycle contributes a small amount of cumulative mechanical and chemical fatigue, progressively reducing the elastane''s ability to fully recover to its original relaxed state, even though this effect isn''t usually visible after just one or a few washes', 'This connects the earlier spandex-production/elastomeric-molecular-network discussion to a real product-lifecycle degradation phenomenon — the same cross-linked elastomeric polymer structure that gives spandex its stretch/recovery is subject to gradual FATIGUE from repeated mechanical stress cycles (stretch-relax during wash agitation) combined with sustained heat/moisture/detergent chemical exposure — this cumulative fatigue, while imperceptible after any single wash, progressively degrades the polymer network''s ability to fully recover its original relaxed shape, which is exactly why garment care instructions (gentler wash cycles, avoiding excessive heat) and elastane draw-ratio/covering-method choices (as discussed earlier) matter for a garment''s LONG-TERM fit retention, not just its initial stretch performance.', 3, '{"elastic-recovery-wash-degradation"}'::text[]),
('0a50ae12-e000-4b92-a5e9-3735064814d2', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A jacquard knitting machine''s individual needle-selection mechanism (electronic, modern version) fails intermittently on ONE specific needle position, causing a repeating small color/pattern error at that exact wale position across the entire fabric roll. Compare this failure mode to the earlier-discussed ''needle line'' MECHANICAL defect (from a bent/worn needle in plain knitting) — what is the KEY diagnostic difference a technologist should look for to distinguish an ELECTRONIC selection fault from a MECHANICAL needle fault at the same recurring wale position?', '["There is no meaningful diagnostic difference; both fault types would look and behave identically, making this distinction technically impossible to determine through any inspection method","A mechanical needle fault (bent hook, worn latch) typically produces a CONSISTENT physical defect signature (e.g., always a slightly different loop SIZE) regardless of the pattern/color being knitted at that position, while an electronic selection fault would show up specifically as WRONG PATTERN/COLOR selection at that position (the needle physically knits fine, but selects the wrong yarn/action), and REPLACING the physical needle would fix a mechanical fault but NOT an electronic selection fault, whereas checking/replacing the needle SELECTOR component (not the needle itself) would be the correct fix for an electronic fault — this distinction guides which specific component to actually replace during troubleshooting","Electronic selection faults can only occur on machines that have zero needles installed, making this comparison scenario technically nonsensical","Mechanical needle faults are always impossible to diagnose through any inspection method, while electronic faults are always immediately obvious through simple visual inspection with no further testing required"]'::jsonb, 'A mechanical needle fault (bent hook, worn latch) typically produces a CONSISTENT physical defect signature (e.g., always a slightly different loop SIZE) regardless of the pattern/color being knitted at that position, while an electronic selection fault would show up specifically as WRONG PATTERN/COLOR selection at that position (the needle physically knits fine, but selects the wrong yarn/action), and REPLACING the physical needle would fix a mechanical fault but NOT an electronic selection fault, whereas checking/replacing the needle SELECTOR component (not the needle itself) would be the correct fix for an electronic fault — this distinction guides which specific component to actually replace during troubleshooting', 'This extends the earlier needle-line defect discussion with a genuinely important modern-machine diagnostic distinction — a MECHANICAL needle defect (bent hook/worn latch) causes a PHYSICAL loop-formation problem independent of what pattern is being knitted (always producing a slightly wrong loop SIZE at that position regardless of color/pattern), while an ELECTRONIC selection fault means the needle mechanically knits FINE but is told to select the WRONG yarn/color/action (a pattern-data or selector-hardware problem, not a physical needle problem) — correctly distinguishing these two fault TYPES at the same recurring wale position is essential because the actual fix differs completely: replacing the physical needle fixes a mechanical fault but does nothing for an electronic selection fault, which instead requires checking/replacing the electronic needle-selector component.', 3, '{"electronic-vs-mechanical-needle-fault"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
